[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A couple of weeks ago we took a look at the Mozart music generation platform and compared it with the latest Suno version 5.5 model. The comments were quite interesting and aligned with my own thoughts. Mozart is indeed very impressive and absolutely a'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contender in the space. But recently they launched their cover feature. How does it compare with Suno\'s cover feature, of which you may know I am a huge fan. So this time we\'re not going'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to play a guessing game on which platform a certain generation came from. We\'re going to generate covers of various kinds in both platforms so that you can hear how each of them handles a particular cover. Today we\'re going to look at covers generated from recordings of just a cappella singing, then also'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'singing with instrumentation, and then finally covers based on instrumentals alone. And with the recent Suno announcements of download limitations, this has a lot of people looking for alternative platforms. And Mozart is rising to the top because of the sheer'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality. If you missed our last video, I invite you to go back and listen to it, but I am seeing Mozart pop up everywhere. I\'m making a bold assumption that most of you know the basics of using one of these music generation platforms, but I will show you how the cover feature is implemented in each'

